1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,990
Lesson 3.4.

2
00:00:02,370 --> 00:00:04,506
Thermodynamic speed limits.

3
00:00:05,617 --> 00:00:08,861
A computer must transform
one probability distribution

4
00:00:08,861 --> 00:00:11,925
into another: from the
initial state to the result.

5
00:00:12,305 --> 00:00:14,650
The question of the course
is whether it can do so

6
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:16,995
fast and with little
dissipation at the same time.

7
00:00:17,375 --> 00:00:22,103
Thermodynamic speed limits answer
precisely: the product of time

8
00:00:22,103 --> 00:00:26,610
and entropy produced is bounded
below by a geometric distance

9
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:30,452
between the distributions,
the Wasserstein distance.

10
00:00:31,583 --> 00:00:34,760
Imagine having to move a pile
of sand from one place to

11
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:37,878
another, with a shovel moving
through a viscous fluid.

12
00:00:38,258 --> 00:00:42,476
The cost of moving sand against friction
grows with the square of the speed.

13
00:00:42,856 --> 00:00:45,730
If we have lots of time,
we go slowly and spend

14
00:00:45,730 --> 00:00:48,604
little; if we have little
time, we spend a lot.

15
00:00:48,984 --> 00:00:52,147
And there is an optimal
way to split the work: move

16
00:00:52,147 --> 00:00:55,185
each grain in a straight
line, at constant speed.

17
00:00:55,565 --> 00:01:00,472
The minimum total distance, measured with
squares, is the Wasserstein distance.

18
00:01:01,583 --> 00:01:09,350
The result, for overdamped
dynamics, is: τ ≥ W₂² / (μ k_B T Σ).

19
00:01:09,730 --> 00:01:14,197
Here W₂ is the Wasserstein distance
between the initial and final

20
00:01:14,197 --> 00:01:18,526
distributions, μ the mobility
and Σ the total entropy produced.

21
00:01:18,906 --> 00:01:24,713
Read backward: Σ ≥ W₂²/(μ k_B T τ).

22
00:01:25,093 --> 00:01:28,642
To cover a fixed distance
in less time, you must pay

23
00:01:28,642 --> 00:01:31,986
more entropy, inversely
proportional to the time.

24
00:01:32,366 --> 00:01:34,424
The curve shows the forbidden region.

25
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:38,946
The Wasserstein distance is the
minimum cost of transporting

26
00:01:38,946 --> 00:01:42,342
one distribution into another,
with cost equal to the square

27
00:01:42,342 --> 00:01:45,002
of the distance traveled
by each grain of mass.

28
00:01:45,382 --> 00:01:49,630
For Gaussians it has a closed
form: the squared difference of

29
00:01:49,630 --> 00:01:53,460
the means plus a covariance
term, the Gelbrich formula.

30
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,823
In one dimension, it is the
squared difference of the means

31
00:01:56,823 --> 00:01:59,604
plus the squared difference
of the standard deviations.

32
00:01:59,984 --> 00:02:02,887
For arbitrary distributions,
that same formula

33
00:02:02,887 --> 00:02:05,663
with means and covariances
is a lower bound.

34
00:02:06,783 --> 00:02:14,592
We define the efficiency as η = W₂² /
(μ τ k_B T Σ), which never exceeds one.

35
00:02:14,972 --> 00:02:18,701
Let's compare two protocols that
translate a particle in a trap.

36
00:02:19,081 --> 00:02:23,685
Dragging the trap at constant
speed: the particle starts with a

37
00:02:23,685 --> 00:02:27,558
lag and its mean accelerates;
the efficiency is 0.88.

38
00:02:27,938 --> 00:02:32,632
The optimal protocol makes the mean
of the distribution move at constant

39
00:02:32,632 --> 00:02:36,869
speed from the very first instant:
the efficiency is exactly one.

40
00:02:37,249 --> 00:02:40,022
The lesson is general:
the optimal protocol

41
00:02:40,022 --> 00:02:42,536
travels the geodesic at constant speed.

42
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:47,718
With stiffness one, mobility
one and k_B T one, we

43
00:02:47,718 --> 00:02:51,623
drag the trap at speed
one for three time units.

44
00:02:52,003 --> 00:02:56,312
The particle's mean advances 2.05 and the

45
00:02:56,312 --> 00:03:00,621
variance does not change, so W₂² is 4.20.

46
00:03:01,001 --> 00:03:06,195
The entropy produced is the integral
of the squared mean velocity, 1.6.

47
00:03:06,575 --> 00:03:13,697
The limit requires at least 4.2 over
three, 1.40; the efficiency is 0.88.

48
00:03:14,077 --> 00:03:16,936
If we wanted the same
transport in half the

49
00:03:16,936 --> 00:03:19,530
time, the minimum entropy would double.

50
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:25,518
For computing this is central: transforming
one distribution into another in a

51
00:03:25,518 --> 00:03:29,949
given time has a minimum dissipation
cost, and erasing fast costs more.

52
00:03:30,329 --> 00:03:33,885
Wasserstein geometry tells
us which protocol is best.

53
00:03:34,265 --> 00:03:38,241
In lab four you will compute the
efficiency of a Gaussian process.

54
00:03:39,350 --> 00:03:43,683
Frequent mistakes: confusing the
Wasserstein distance with work,

55
00:03:43,683 --> 00:03:47,949
forgetting the system entropy,
applying the overdamped bound to

56
00:03:47,949 --> 00:03:52,553
systems with inertia, and believing
ideal efficiency comes for free.

57
00:03:53,683 --> 00:03:57,575
In summary: transforming
distributions costs

58
00:03:57,575 --> 00:04:00,847
entropy, at least W₂² over μ k_B T τ.

59
00:04:01,227 --> 00:04:03,811
Fast and efficient at once is impossible.

60
00:04:04,191 --> 00:04:07,203
The Wasserstein distance
measures how much must be

61
00:04:07,203 --> 00:04:10,637
transported, the Gelbrich
formula gives it for Gaussians,

62
00:04:10,637 --> 00:04:13,890
and the optimal protocol
transports at constant speed.

63
00:04:15,017 --> 00:04:19,030
To practice: compute a Wasserstein
distance between Gaussians,

64
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:23,109
the minimum entropy of a translation,
prove the inequality with

65
00:04:23,109 --> 00:04:26,346
Cauchy–Schwarz, and describe
the optimal protocol.
